From a5d35ed8f512b997232b10145afe4dda832df151 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 11 Aug 2026 01:19:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] P6-printing-module-product-print-config-composer
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Printing Module, Phase 6 (product print-configuration composer + GET/PUT sub-resource,
decision Q-07):

- ProductPrintConfigComposer: ValidateAsync (payload-shape matching against UsingPrinterType,
  ribbon-type existence, HEX-color regex per module requirement §3), StageForProductAsync,
  ReplaceForProductAsync (same-family update-in-place or Q-08 family switch: hard-delete old +
  insert new), SoftDeleteForProductAsync / RestoreForProductAsync (cascade with the owning
  product, same reasoning as the printer registry in P5).
- ProductPrintConfigurationService: GetForProductAsync (tenant-scoped, open to both roles) /
  UpdateForProductAsync (system-admin only, confirmed Q-09). Built on the composer, mirroring
  TransferService/ITransferComposer.
- ProductPrintConfigController: GET/PUT api/products/{id}/print-config, no standalone
  POST/DELETE per decision Q-07.
- IServiceManager / ServiceManager / DI: IProductPrintConfigurationService (+ the composer)
  wired through (14/14 service property parity verified by script).
- New error PrintingErrors.ProductPrintConfigActorNotResolved, matching the established
  per-module pattern (Batch/BranchRequest/Printer/PrintImage each have their own). 27/27 error
  codes now have full EN/AR resx parity.

Domain/Infrastructure corrections needed before the composer could actually work (metadata
only - no new columns, no migration beyond what P1 already produced), same class of issue P5
hit with the printer registry:
- MaticaProductPrintConfiguration.Product and EvolisProductPrintConfiguration.Product
  navigation properties added. IProductPrintConfigComposer.StageForProductAsync's signature
  changed from (long productId) to (Product product): a product created in the same
  transaction as its print configuration has no real id yet at staging time, and
  ExecuteInTransactionAsync's contract forbids calling SaveChanges early to get it. The
  navigation lets EF Core fix up the FK once the product's identity is generated within the
  one SaveChanges call. Safe to change now - nothing outside this composer implementation
  consumed the old signature yet.
- IMaticaProductPrintConfigRepo / IEvolisProductPrintConfigRepo: added
  GetByProductIdIncludingDeletedAsync. RestoreForProductAsync needs it - the filtered
  GetByProductIdForUpdateAsync can never find a row that is, by definition, currently
  soft-deleted, so restore would silently no-op forever without this.
- IProductPrintConfigComposer.SoftDeleteForProductAsync gained an actorId parameter so the
  child configuration row's DeletedBy matches the parent product's instead of always being null.

Bug caught and fixed before commit (not after): ProductPrintConfigurationService.
UpdateForProductAsync originally built its response by re-querying the database
(BuildResponseAsync, AsNoTracking) from inside the same ExecuteInTransactionAsync delegate that
had just staged the change. Since ExecuteInTransactionAsync only calls SaveChanges after the
delegate returns, that query would have hit the database directly and seen pre-update values on
an edit, or nothing at all on a first-time create - every successful update would have returned
either stale data or a spurious 404. Replaced with MapValidatedResponseAsync, which builds the
response from the in-memory validated entities the composer just staged, matching the pattern
PrinterConfigurationService (P5) already used correctly for the same situation.
---
